  "query_preview": "..\nTonight through Wednesday: Dry and mild weather is forecast.\nLimited cloud cover this afternoon and evening will give way\nto thickening middle and high clouds later tonight into Monday.\nLow pressure offshore digs south and draws some moisture over \nthe region to bring these clouds. However the deeper moisture \nand better lift stay south, so the forecast remains dry. Clouds \ndecrease going into Tuesday and Wednesday. Wind will be breezy \nTuesday in the northerly flow through the Okanogan Valley to \nWaterville Plateau, with the deeper low to the south. Gusts of \n15-25 mph are possible. Otherwise winds will be lighter. Highs \nwill largely in the 60s Monday, warming to the mid-60s to low \n70s Tuesday and Wednesday. Low will be the 30s to low 40s, with \nsome upper 20s in sheltered valley. \n\nWednesday night to Sunday: Low pressure continues to dig into\nCalifornia, while another trough with a more northerly track \napproaches the area and starts to draw moisture in. Clouds \nthicken and rain chances start to enter southeast WA/lower ID \nWednesday overnight into Thursday. The best chances will be over\nsoutheast WA/lower ID but at least a moderate chances (30-50%) \nextends toward northeast WA and north ID. This will largely be\ndue to moisture coming in from the south and wave moving along a\nboundary near the southeast CWA. Other rain chances will be \nfound near the Cascades later Thursday into Thursday night with\nthe approach of that northern system. The best chance of wetting\nrain (>=0.10\") between Thursday and Thursday night is over \nsoutheast WA through the central and southern ID Panhandle at \n50-70%.\n\nThereafter we will have to watch how systems and moisture \ninteract with the incoming northern system. Friday right now \nlooks drier, save for some shower chances near the mountains. \nHowever westerly winds increase over the region with a wave \nmoving in with gusts of 15-30 mph, stronger over the upper \nColumbia Basin to Waterville Plateau. With the incoming low the \nrain chances expand back out later Saturday into next Sunday, \nalong with cooler temperatures. The spread between the 25th to\n75th percentile in terms of high temperature remains modestly\nwide at 10-15 degrees and there is also a spread in snow levels,\nmeaning confidence in the details wanes. With that said, cooler\ntemperatures and lower snow levels are forecast Friday into\nSunday which could bring them down to pass level. It could even\nbe cold enough Saturday night into next Sunday AM for some\nrain/snow mix in some of the northern valleys and maybe around\nthe metro. The chance of wetting rain (>=0.10\") Saturday night\nis around 20-40%, lowest in the deeper basin; chances rise to \naround 30-50% Sunday locally near 60% in the Cascades. This is \nall to say, rain amounts are not only trace amounts but also not\nvery heavy at this point. Snow chances come to the Cascades \nSaturday and starts to expand to the other mountains Saturday \nnight into Sunday. In the 48-hour period between Saturday and \nSunday night, models currently show about 2 inches of snow in \nthe passes, locally around 3 to 4 possible at Sherman and \nWashington Pass. Right snow probabilities for 1\" is around \n30-40, near 60% near Washington Pass.\n\nHighs stay in the 60s Thursday, then cool to the 50s Friday into\nSunday, locally the upper 40s Sunday. Lows will be in 30s to low\n40s Thursday night/Friday AM, then upper 20s and 30s \nthereafter, with Saturday morning the coldest morning. Friday \nlooks breezy across the region with west-northwest gusting \n15-30 mph; next Sunday also has similarly breezy conditions \nover central WA. /Solveig",
